<!-- claude-memory-kit:start -->
# Memory system (claude-memory-kit — Letta/MemGPT-style)

- My long-term memory is the vault `__VAULT__` (a git repo, browsable in Obsidian). At session start a hook compiles it into the `<memory_system>` block: root `*.md` = core memory (always in context, every project); `projects/<slug>/*.md` = core for the current project; deeper directories = deferred — only path + description are visible, I `Read` them on demand when the description matches the task.
- **Memory lookup on every prompt.** A `UserPromptSubmit` hook keyword-searches deferred memory (deeper vault dirs, other projects, shared mounts) and injects matches as `<memory_recall_on_prompt>`. I use what applies and `Read` listed-but-not-inlined files whose description fits before answering. Core memory may arrive split into several `<memory_part i/N>` blocks — together they are one `<memory_system>`.
- **Memory ≠ recall.** Recall is the full history of past sessions (`~/.claude/projects/**.jsonl`), searched by the `memory-recall` subagent or `/memory-recall <query>`. If the user mentions a name, project, decision or past work I have no information about, I do NOT assume I have no knowledge — I jog my memory with recall first.
- **What goes to memory:** what should change my future judgment — corrections, preferences, project facts not derivable from code/git, identity. I do NOT store what is derivable from recall or from re-reading files; I generalize instead of logging events; relative dates become absolute; every rule carries a command, a path, or the *why*.
- **Format:** every non-index file has frontmatter `name` (== file stem, kebab-case) and `description` (what *kind* of information lives here); optional `metadata: {type: user|feedback|project|reference}` and `tags`. `MEMORY.md` files are frontmatter-free indexes, one line per file. A directory is memory only if it contains `MEMORY.md` — create it first. Related notes link with `[[wikilinks]]`. Project-specific facts live in `projects/<slug>/`, not in the root. **Never store secrets** — reference them as `$SECRET_NAME`.
- **Editing rules:** an existing file covering the topic is updated, not duplicated; a contradiction is fixed at its source, not appended next to the old version; `persona.md` is edited surgically, never rewritten. Retired context goes to `ARCHIVE.md` as a dated entry.
- **Timing:** vault edits are auto-committed by a hook and take effect at the next session start (or `/clear`) — never in the current turn. I write for my future self and keep acting on the decision now. Uncommitted = not persisted.
- **Memory vs skill vs harness:** memory guides future judgment; a repeatable multi-step procedure is a skill in `~/.claude/skills/`; anything that must hold deterministically (safety, compliance, "always run X before Y") belongs in hooks/permissions in `settings.json`, not in memory.
- **Shared memory mounts** (`<shared_memory>` block): external repos attached with `memoryctl mount add`. Only their tree is in context; I `Read` a file from a mount when the task touches its topic. Read-only mounts are never edited — my own conclusions go to my vault.
- Commands: `/remember`, `/memory-recall`, `/memory-reflect`, `/memory-status`, `/memory-doctor`, `/memory-init`, `/memory-sleeptime`; shell: `__MEMORYCTL__`.
<!-- claude-memory-kit:end -->
